BANK.push(
{"id":"n1","d":3,"q":"A manufacturing company is connecting its plant-floor control systems to a cloud analytics platform so engineers can monitor equipment performance. The control network currently has no connection to the corporate network. The OT manager insists that nothing on the internet must ever be able to initiate a connection into the control network. Which architecture BEST meets the requirement?","o":["Send data outbound through an industrial DMZ using a data diode or unidirectional gateway","Join the control network to the corporate network behind a next-generation firewall that permits only the cloud provider's published IP ranges inbound","Build a site-to-site IPsec VPN from the control network straight to the cloud provider","Give engineers remote desktop access to a control-network jump host protected by MFA and a commercial VPN client"],"a":[0],"e":"A unidirectional gateway physically enforces one-way flow, so nothing can initiate inbound. An IDMZ adds a buffer between OT and IT. Firewalls, VPNs, and remote access all allow some inbound path that can be misconfigured or abused."},
{"id":"n2","d":3,"q":"An organization is replacing its legacy hub-and-spoke VPN with a design based on zero trust principles. Remote users currently get full network access once connected. Which design change delivers the GREATEST reduction in lateral movement risk?","o":["Broker access to individual applications based on identity, device posture, and policy, without putting users on the network","Raise VPN encryption from AES-128 to AES-256 and switch the VPN to certificate-based authentication for every remote user","Add an inline IPS behind the VPN concentrator to inspect remote users' traffic","Split-tunnel the VPN so that only corporate traffic uses the tunnel"],"a":[0],"e":"Zero trust network access (ZTNA) gives per-application access instead of network-level access, so a compromised endpoint can't scan or reach other systems. Stronger encryption and IPS help but still place users on the network, and split tunneling doesn't reduce lateral movement."},
{"id":"n3","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing key management for a large cloud deployment that encrypts petabytes of data. The design uses a key encryption key (KEK) in an HSM-backed KMS to wrap per-object data encryption keys (DEKs) stored alongside the data. What is the PRIMARY benefit of this envelope encryption design?","o":["Protected KEK, fast local DEKs, and cheap KEK rotation","Access controls on the stored objects are no longer needed, because every DEK is encrypted and useless without the KEK","The cloud provider's support engineers can read data for troubleshooting without needing any extra keys","It replaces symmetric encryption with asymmetric encryption, which performs better on very large data sets"],"a":[0],"e":"Envelope encryption scales: the KEK stays protected, DEKs do the bulk work, and KEK rotation means re-wrapping small DEKs. Access control is still needed (KMS policies), and asymmetric encryption is far slower for bulk data."},
{"id":"n4","d":3,"q":"A company's cryptography standard requires planning for post-quantum risk. The architect is told that adversaries may be recording encrypted traffic today to decrypt later. Which data is MOST at risk from this 'harvest now, decrypt later' threat?","o":["Long-lived confidential data exchanged using RSA or elliptic-curve key agreement","Data at rest encrypted with AES-256 under properly managed keys in a hardware-backed key store","Short-lived session tokens that expire 15 minutes after they are issued","Password hashes stored using Argon2id with a unique random salt for each password"],"a":[0],"e":"Shor's algorithm breaks RSA and ECC, so recorded key exchanges could be broken later to reveal session keys. Data that must stay secret for years is most exposed. AES-256 retains adequate strength against Grover's algorithm, expired tokens lose value, and password hashing isn't based on public-key math."},
{"id":"n5","d":3,"q":"An architect must select a post-quantum migration approach for TLS on internet-facing services. Which approach is MOST appropriate today?","o":["Hybrid key exchange combining a classical algorithm with a NIST-standardized post-quantum KEM, plus crypto-agility","Replace TLS everywhere with a proprietary quantum-resistant protocol from a single specialist vendor","Increase RSA keys to 8,192 bits, which makes them resistant to quantum attacks","Make no changes until large-scale quantum computers are publicly demonstrated, then migrate all services at once using the standards available at that time"],"a":[0],"e":"Hybrid key exchange (for example, X25519MLKEM768) protects against both classical and quantum attacks during transition. Larger RSA keys don't resist Shor's algorithm, proprietary protocols lack scrutiny, and waiting leaves recorded data exposed."},
{"id":"n6","d":3,"q":"A security architect is reviewing a design for an internal PKI. The root CA is planned to be online, domain-joined, and used to issue end-entity certificates directly. What is the BEST recommendation?","o":["An offline, HSM-protected root that signs only subordinate issuing CAs and CRLs, with the issuing CAs online for daily use","Keep the root online and domain-joined, but harden it with a host-based firewall, application allow-listing, and endpoint antivirus","Keep the design but reduce the root certificate's validity period to one year","Replace the internal PKI with self-signed certificates on each server"],"a":[0],"e":"A two-tier hierarchy with an offline root limits exposure of the most critical key. If an issuing CA is compromised, the root can revoke it. An online root issuing end-entity certificates is a single point of catastrophic failure, and self-signed certificates destroy centralized trust."},
{"id":"n7","d":3,"q":"An e-commerce company's website was taken offline by a 400 Gbps volumetric DDoS attack that saturated its ISP links. The company has a next-generation firewall and an on-premises DDoS appliance. What architectural change BEST protects against a repeat?","o":["Use an upstream cloud scrubbing service or CDN with large distributed capacity","Upgrade the on-premises DDoS appliance to a model rated for much higher throughput and more simultaneous connections","Add firewall rules that block the source IP addresses observed during the attack","Increase the ISP links from 10 Gbps to 40 Gbps each"],"a":[0],"e":"Volumetric attacks must be stopped upstream, since anything on-premises sits behind already saturated links. Cloud scrubbing and CDNs have terabits of capacity. Appliances, firewall rules, and modest bandwidth increases can't absorb 400 Gbps."},
{"id":"n8","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing DNS security for an organization. Attackers recently poisoned the cache of a recursive resolver used by staff, redirecting them to a phishing site. Which control MOST directly prevents this?","o":["DNSSEC validation on the resolvers","Enable DNS over HTTPS in all client browsers so that the queries between clients and the resolver are encrypted and can't be read","Move all DNS servers behind the internal firewall","Reduce the DNS cache TTL to 60 seconds"],"a":[0],"e":"DNSSEC adds origin authentication and integrity to DNS data, so validating resolvers reject forged records. DoH encrypts the client-to-resolver hop but doesn't stop poisoning of the resolver itself. TTL reduction only shortens the window."},
{"id":"n9","d":3,"q":"An organization wants to protect its email domain from being spoofed in phishing emails sent to customers. Which combination of controls BEST accomplishes this?","o":["SPF, DKIM, and DMARC at p=reject","A secure email gateway that scans all inbound messages for malware, malicious links, and impersonation of executives","Mandatory TLS on all outbound email connections","Training customers to check the sender's display name"],"a":[0],"e":"SPF and DKIM authenticate sending sources and message integrity, and DMARC tells receivers to reject mail that fails alignment. Monitoring first avoids blocking legitimate mail. A gateway protects inbound mail, TLS encrypts transport, and display names are trivially spoofed."},
{"id":"n10","d":3,"q":"A hospital is designing its wireless network. Medical devices only support WPA2-Personal, while staff laptops support WPA3-Enterprise. What is the BEST architecture?","o":["Separate SSIDs and segments by device capability, isolating the medical devices","A single WPA2-Personal SSID for all devices, so every device can connect","A single WPA3-Enterprise SSID, replacing any medical devices that can't connect","An open SSID with a captive portal for the medical devices"],"a":[0],"e":"Segmenting by capability lets each class use its strongest supported option, and isolating weaker devices limits the impact of PSK compromise. Lowest-common-denominator security weakens everything, replacing devices may be impractical, and open SSIDs offer no encryption."},
{"id":"n11","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing network segmentation for a data center that hosts thousands of workloads that change frequently. Traditional VLAN and firewall rule changes take weeks. Which approach BEST provides fine-grained segmentation that keeps pace with change?","o":["Workload-level microsegmentation","A separate VLAN for each application, with all traffic routed through a core firewall that holds every inter-application rule","A single flat network relying on host-based antivirus","A network IDS attached to the core switch's SPAN port"],"a":[0],"e":"Microsegmentation applies policy based on workload identity or labels, so it follows workloads as they move or scale. VLAN sprawl doesn't scale, flat networks allow lateral movement, and IDS detects but doesn't segment."},
{"id":"n12","d":3,"q":"A cloud architect is designing the network for a multi-account cloud environment. Security wants central inspection of traffic between workload accounts and to the internet. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Hub-and-spoke with a central inspection hub that all spoke traffic must cross","Full-mesh peering between every account, with security groups as the only control between workloads in different accounts","A separate internet gateway in every account with no central controls","A single account for all workloads to simplify networking"],"a":[0],"e":"Hub-and-spoke with a transit or inspection hub centralizes egress and east-west inspection. Full mesh bypasses inspection and grows exponentially, decentralized gateways lose visibility, and one account loses isolation."},
{"id":"n13","d":3,"q":"An organization stores customer data in a cloud object storage service. A misconfigured bucket was recently found to be publicly readable. Which control BEST prevents recurrence across the organization?","o":["An organization-level guardrail blocking public access, plus CSPM drift monitoring","Mandatory cloud security training for every developer, with a short quiz on storage permissions at the end of each module","Renaming buckets with random strings so they are harder to find","Turning on server-side encryption for every bucket"],"a":[0],"e":"A preventive organization-wide guardrail (such as an account-level block on public access or a service control policy) stops the misconfiguration at its source, and CSPM catches drift. Training helps but isn't enforcement, random names are obscurity, and encryption at rest doesn't stop authorized public reads."},
{"id":"n14","d":3,"q":"A security architect must decide on the cloud service model for a new application. The organization wants the provider to manage the operating system and runtime, while the organization controls the application code and data. Which model fits, and which security responsibility REMAINS with the organization?","o":["PaaS; the organization still owns application security, data protection, and identity and access configuration","IaaS; the organization is responsible only for configuring the virtual network","SaaS; the organization remains responsible for patching the operating system","PaaS; the provider takes responsibility for every aspect of security, including the security of the organization's own application code"],"a":[0],"e":"In PaaS the provider manages infrastructure, OS, and runtime. The customer is responsible for code, data, identities, and access configuration. Data and access are always the customer's responsibility in every model."},
{"id":"n15","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing container security for a Kubernetes platform. Which control MOST effectively limits the impact of a compromised container on the host and other workloads?","o":["Least-privilege runtime settings, enforced at admission","Larger images with more debugging tools for faster response","Privileged mode for all containers, so that host-level monitoring agents can fully inspect every running process","Host network namespace for all pods to simplify network policy"],"a":[0],"e":"Least privilege at runtime limits escape and lateral movement. Admission control enforces it consistently. Privileged mode and host networking remove isolation, and larger images increase attack surface."},
{"id":"n16","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing endpoint protection for 50,000 laptops. The CISO wants to detect novel attacks such as living-off-the-land techniques that use legitimate tools. Which capability is MOST important?","o":["EDR with behavioral analytics, retained telemetry, and central investigation","Signature-based antivirus updated every hour from the vendor's cloud, with heuristic scanning enabled on all file types","Full-disk encryption on all laptops","A host firewall blocking all inbound connections"],"a":[0],"e":"Living-off-the-land attacks use legitimate binaries (PowerShell, WMI), so signatures don't help. EDR's behavioral detection and telemetry enable detection and investigation. Encryption and firewalls are protective, not detective."},
{"id":"n17","d":3,"q":"An organization is designing a secure remote administration architecture for critical servers. Which design is MOST secure?","o":["Privileged workstations to a monitored PAM bastion with MFA and JIT","Direct RDP from administrators' everyday laptops over the corporate VPN","A shared jump server using one shared local administrator password","SSH with password authentication exposed to the internet on a non-standard port"],"a":[0],"e":"PAWs prevent credential theft from everyday endpoints, the bastion or PAM gateway centralizes control and recording, and JIT credentials limit standing privilege. The other options expose credentials or lack accountability."},
{"id":"n18","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing the encryption architecture for a database containing payment card numbers. The application needs to search by card number for customer service lookups. Which design BEST balances security and functionality?","o":["Tokenize card numbers in a separate vault and look them up by token or keyed hash","Store card numbers in plaintext and restrict database access using long, complex database account passwords rotated quarterly","Encrypt the whole database with transparent data encryption only","Hash card numbers with unsalted SHA-256 so lookups still work"],"a":[0],"e":"Tokenization removes card numbers from the main database and narrows PCI scope. A keyed hash allows equality lookups without exposing values. TDE protects only against media theft, and unsalted hashes of 16-digit PANs can be brute-forced quickly."},
{"id":"n19","d":3,"q":"An architect needs to protect data in use while running analytics on sensitive data in a public cloud, so that even a compromised hypervisor cannot read the data. Which technology BEST addresses this?","o":["Confidential computing using hardware trusted execution environments with memory encryption and remote attestation","Full-disk encryption of the virtual machine's boot and data volumes using keys from the provider's KMS","TLS between the analytics client and the virtual machine","A cloud firewall restricting inbound traffic to the VM"],"a":[0],"e":"Confidential computing (for example, AMD SEV-SNP, Intel TDX) encrypts memory and isolates the workload from the hypervisor and host. Disk encryption protects data at rest, TLS protects data in transit, and firewalls don't stop a malicious host."},
{"id":"n20","d":3,"q":"A company wants to securely connect 300 branch offices to its data centers and cloud services, with consistent security policy and direct internet breakout at branches. Which architecture BEST meets these requirements?","o":["SD-WAN for connectivity, combined with a cloud-delivered security service edge (SASE) for policy, inspection, and ZTNA","MPLS circuits to every branch, with all internet traffic backhauled to the headquarters firewall for inspection","Site-to-site VPNs between every pair of branches","Broadband with consumer routers and no central policy"],"a":[0],"e":"SASE combines SD-WAN with cloud-delivered security (SWG, CASB, ZTNA, FWaaS), giving consistent policy with local breakout. Backhauling adds latency and cost, full-mesh VPNs don't scale, and unmanaged routers have no policy."},
{"id":"n21","d":3,"q":"A regional bank runs its core banking system in a primary data center. Regulators require an RTO of 2 hours and an RPO of 5 minutes. The secondary site is 400 km away. Which replication design BEST meets these objectives without unacceptable latency on production transactions?","o":["Asynchronous replication with short intervals, plus automated, regularly tested failover runbooks","Synchronous replication to the secondary site, so that no committed transaction can ever be lost, whatever the distance between sites","Nightly backups shipped to the secondary site by courier","Weekly full backups to the cloud with daily incrementals"],"a":[0],"e":"At 400 km, synchronous replication adds round-trip latency to every write and can hurt performance. Asynchronous replication with lag kept under 5 minutes meets the RPO, and tested automated failover meets the 2-hour RTO. Nightly or weekly backups can't meet a 5-minute RPO."},
{"id":"n22","d":3,"q":"An architect is asked to design protection against ransomware for the organization's backup infrastructure. Attackers in recent incidents deleted backups using stolen domain administrator credentials before encrypting production. Which design element is MOST important?","o":["Immutable backups in a separate identity domain","AES-256 encryption of every backup set, with the encryption key stored securely on the backup server itself","Hourly instead of daily backups","Backups on the same SAN as production for faster restore"],"a":[0],"e":"Attackers target backups first. Immutability (WORM or object lock) and separation from the production identity domain mean stolen domain credentials can't destroy them. Encryption protects confidentiality, not deletion. More frequent backups are still deletable, and same-SAN storage shares the failure domain."},
{"id":"n23","d":3,"q":"A company is designing a secure architecture for IoT sensors deployed in public locations. Devices could be physically captured and analyzed by attackers. Which design decision MOST limits the impact of a single captured device?","o":["Give each device a unique identity and credentials held in a secure element","Use one strong pre-shared key across the fleet, stored in encrypted flash memory with the decryption key derived from the device's serial number","Disable remote firmware updates entirely","Rely on tamper-resistant enclosures alone"],"a":[0],"e":"Unique per-device credentials in a secure element mean one capture compromises only one device, and that device can be revoked. A shared key breaks every device at once. Disabling updates prevents patching, and enclosures alone delay but don't prevent extraction."},
{"id":"n24","d":3,"q":"An architect is evaluating TLS configurations for internal services. A legacy application only supports TLS 1.0 with CBC-mode ciphers. The business cannot replace it for a year. What is the BEST approach?","o":["A modern TLS proxy in front, legacy leg isolated","Allow TLS 1.0 across the entire organization so that the legacy application keeps working with all clients","Disable TLS for the application and rely on segmentation","Edit the application's config files to add modern ciphers"],"a":[0],"e":"A proxy lets clients use modern TLS while the legacy hop is confined to a protected segment, a common compensating control. Enabling TLS 1.0 broadly weakens everything, removing TLS exposes data, and you can't add ciphers the application doesn't support."},
{"id":"n25","d":3,"q":"A security architect is choosing a hashing approach for storing user passwords in a new identity system. Which option is BEST?","o":["A memory-hard password hashing function such as Argon2id, with a unique salt per password and tuned work factors","SHA-256 with a single global salt applied to every password, so that precomputed rainbow tables can't be used","MD5 with a unique salt per password","AES-256 encryption with the key in the app's configuration"],"a":[0],"e":"Password storage needs a slow, salted, memory-hard function to resist GPU cracking. Argon2id is recommended (bcrypt and scrypt are acceptable). Fast hashes like SHA-256 and MD5 are unsuitable, and reversible encryption means a key leak exposes every password."},
{"id":"n26","d":3,"q":"A multinational company needs secure communication between its offices in countries that restrict the use of strong encryption. The architect must comply with local laws while protecting data. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Get legal advice per country and route sensitive data accordingly","Use the strongest available encryption everywhere and disregard local restrictions","Disable encryption on all traffic to and from those countries","Use steganography to hide the encrypted traffic"],"a":[0],"e":"Some countries regulate the import or use of encryption. Legal review and architecture that avoids sending sensitive data through restrictive jurisdictions balances compliance with protection. Ignoring the law creates legal risk, and steganography to evade law is not an acceptable design."},
{"id":"n27","d":3,"q":"A data center houses critical systems. A physical security assessment found that the loading dock connects directly to the server room corridor, and delivery personnel sometimes walk through unescorted. Which control BEST addresses this?","o":["A controlled delivery staging area outside the secure zone","Add more CCTV cameras covering the corridor from the loading dock to the server room","Post signs stating that unauthorized entry is prohibited","Require delivery staff to sign a confidentiality agreement"],"a":[0],"e":"Layered physical security separates public and secure zones, with controlled transitions. CCTV is detective, signs are deterrent, and agreements don't prevent physical access."},
{"id":"n28","d":3,"q":"An organization has adopted a 'cloud-first' strategy and uses dozens of SaaS applications, many of them adopted without IT approval. Which technology BEST gives visibility into and control over SaaS usage and data movement?","o":["A CASB using log-based discovery plus API or inline enforcement and DLP","A traditional on-premises firewall with URL category filtering enabled for all outbound web traffic from the office network","A network IDS on the internet uplink","Endpoint antivirus on every laptop"],"a":[0],"e":"CASB discovers shadow SaaS, assesses risk, and enforces data policies through APIs and proxies. Firewalls and IDS have limited visibility into SaaS activity, and antivirus doesn't govern SaaS data."},
{"id":"n29","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing a network for a payment card environment. Which approach MOST effectively isolates the cardholder data environment (CDE) while allowing required business flows?","o":["A default-deny CDE segment, validated by testing","Keep the CDE on the corporate VLAN, protected by host-based firewalls","VLAN separation between CDE and corporate VLANs, without filtering","Total physical separation with no connectivity to any other network, including the networks that business processes rely on"],"a":[0],"e":"PCI requires segmentation that is actually restrictive (default deny, justified flows) and verified by testing. VLANs without filtering aren't segmentation, and total isolation usually breaks needed business flows."},
{"id":"n30","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing virtualization security for a private cloud. Which risk is UNIQUE to virtualized environments compared with physical servers?","o":["VM escape into the hypervisor and other guests","SQL injection in a web application hosted on one of the virtual machines in the private cloud","Weak passwords on guest operating systems","Unpatched applications running inside the guests"],"a":[0],"e":"VM escape is specific to virtualization: one guest compromising the hypervisor and every other guest on it. SQL injection, weak passwords, and missing patches occur on physical servers too."},
{"id":"n31","d":3,"q":"An organization is deploying an intrusion prevention system inline at its internet edge. The business is concerned that false positives will block legitimate customer transactions. What is the BEST deployment approach?","o":["Deploy in detection-only mode first, tune against observed traffic, then enable blocking for high-confidence signatures in stages","Enable every signature in blocking mode immediately, to get the maximum protection from day one","Run the IPS out-of-band permanently and never enable blocking","Disable any signature that has ever produced a false positive in any customer environment, according to the vendor's telemetry"],"a":[0],"e":"Staged deployment with tuning reduces business impact while gaining protection. Full blocking on day one risks outages, permanent detect-only gives up prevention, and disabling signatures wholesale creates gaps."},
{"id":"n32","d":3,"q":"A company's network team proposes using 802.1X with EAP-TLS for wired network access control. What is the PRIMARY security benefit?","o":["Devices must present a valid certificate before getting network access at the port","All traffic on the wired network is encrypted end to end between every pair of hosts, including traffic within the same VLAN","Users no longer need to authenticate to applications","The network is protected from volumetric DDoS attacks"],"a":[0],"e":"802.1X authenticates devices or users at the port before granting access, and EAP-TLS uses mutual certificates (strong, resists credential phishing). It doesn't encrypt traffic (MACsec does), replace application authentication, or stop DDoS."},
{"id":"n33","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing storage for highly sensitive research data on a shared SAN. Which control MOST directly prevents a compromised server from accessing LUNs assigned to other servers?","o":["LUN masking and zoning","RAID 6 protection on every disk group in the array, so that two simultaneous drive failures don't cause data loss","Thin provisioning to reduce wasted capacity","Hourly snapshots of each LUN"],"a":[0],"e":"Zoning (at the fabric) and LUN masking (at the array) control which hosts can see which storage. RAID protects against disk failure, thin provisioning is capacity management, and snapshots support recovery, not access control."},
{"id":"n34","d":3,"q":"A security architect is evaluating cloud key management options. The organization requires FIPS 140-3 Level 3 protection for key material and single-tenant control over the HSM. Which option BEST fits?","o":["A dedicated, single-tenant cloud HSM validated to FIPS 140-3 Level 3","The provider's default multi-tenant KMS with provider-managed keys, which is validated to FIPS 140-3 and rotates keys automatically each year","Keys in an encrypted config file on the application server","A software key vault running inside a container"],"a":[0],"e":"Dedicated cloud HSMs provide single-tenant, validated hardware with customer-exclusive key control. Multi-tenant KMS may have suitable validation but isn't single-tenant, and software storage doesn't meet Level 3."},
{"id":"n35","d":3,"q":"A company plans to let employees use personal smartphones to access corporate email and documents (BYOD). Employees refuse to let the company fully control their devices. Which architecture BEST balances security and privacy?","o":["App-level management with selective wipe","Full device enrollment, with the ability to wipe the whole device and read personal messages if needed","No controls, since the devices belong to the employees","Block all mobile access to corporate email and documents, and provide employees with company-owned laptops for remote work instead"],"a":[0],"e":"MAM protects corporate data inside managed apps without controlling the whole device, and selective wipe removes only corporate data. Full device control conflicts with privacy, no controls risks data, and blocking access hurts productivity."},
{"id":"n36","d":3,"q":"An organization uses network address translation at its edge and believes this provides adequate security for internal hosts. Which statement BEST corrects this misconception?","o":["NAT is not a security control","NAT encrypts traffic between internal hosts and the internet","NAT prevents all inbound and outbound attacks","NAT removes the need for a separate firewall in IPv6 networks, because every internal host is hidden behind a single translated address"],"a":[0],"e":"NAT's stateful translation incidentally blocks unsolicited inbound connections, but it isn't designed as a security control: no inspection, no egress policy, no protection from phishing or malicious downloads. IPv6 often runs without NAT, so a firewall is still essential."},
{"id":"n37","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing a highly available firewall pair for a data center edge. Which design consideration is MOST important for maintaining security during a failover?","o":["Stateful failover with synchronized session and configuration state, so connections persist and policy stays consistent","Two firewalls from different vendors in the pair, so a vulnerability in one product can't affect the other","Configuring the secondary to allow all traffic during failover","Disabling logging on the secondary to improve performance"],"a":[0],"e":"State synchronization keeps sessions alive and policies identical so failover doesn't drop or open traffic. Vendor diversity complicates HA pairing, fail-open undermines security, and disabling logging loses visibility."},
{"id":"n38","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing the architecture for a remote site with an unreliable internet connection. The site must keep authenticating users when disconnected from headquarters. Which design is BEST?","o":["A read-only DC or limited credential cache on site","Allow users to log in without authentication whenever the WAN link to headquarters is down","A shared local account for all site users during outages","Require users to wait until the WAN is restored, and have managers record work done manually on paper until then"],"a":[0],"e":"An RODC or limited credential cache supports local authentication while limiting exposure if the site is compromised. Unauthenticated or shared accounts remove accountability, and waiting harms operations."},
{"id":"n39","d":3,"q":"A network architect proposes using IPsec in transport mode for all server-to-server communication in the data center. What does transport mode protect, compared with tunnel mode?","o":["Transport mode protects the payload but keeps the original IP header; tunnel mode protects the whole original packet","Transport mode encrypts only the headers, while tunnel mode encrypts only the payload","Transport mode provides only authentication, while tunnel mode provides only encryption","There is no difference in what is protected; the modes differ only in which devices terminate the security association"],"a":[0],"e":"Transport mode is typical for host-to-host communication: the payload is protected and original headers are kept. Tunnel mode wraps the entire packet in a new IP header, used for gateway-to-gateway VPNs. Both can use AH or ESP."},
{"id":"n40","d":3,"q":"A company stores sensitive engineering drawings in a document management system. It wants protection to persist even after documents are downloaded and emailed outside the company. Which technology BEST achieves this?","o":["Information rights management that encrypts each document and enforces usage policy wherever it travels","Network DLP at the email gateway that blocks any outbound message containing engineering drawings","Full-disk encryption on employee laptops","Strong ACLs on the document management system"],"a":[0],"e":"IRM travels with the document, enforcing policy wherever it goes. DLP and ACLs only control the boundary or repository, and full-disk encryption protects only the laptop's disk."},
{"id":"n41","d":3,"q":"An organization's security architect is reviewing the design of a new data center network that uses software-defined networking (SDN). Which component, if compromised, has the GREATEST impact?","o":["The SDN controller, because it programs forwarding behavior and security policy across the entire network fabric","A single top-of-rack switch that connects a full rack of production servers to the data center fabric","An individual server's network interface card","A single access-layer switch port"],"a":[0],"e":"The controller is the centralized brain of SDN: compromise lets an attacker redirect, intercept, or drop traffic network-wide. It needs strong authentication, isolation, high availability, and protection of northbound and southbound APIs."},
{"id":"n42","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing log transport from thousands of devices to a central SIEM. Some logs contain sensitive data, and the network between sites is untrusted. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["TLS with mutual authentication to collectors, with local buffering during outages","Plain UDP syslog to the SIEM, which gives the best performance for very high event volumes from thousands of devices","Nightly email of log files to the SIEM team","Keeping logs only locally on each device"],"a":[0],"e":"TLS with mutual authentication protects confidentiality and integrity in transit and prevents spoofed log sources, and local buffering prevents gaps. UDP syslog is unencrypted and lossy, emailing logs is slow and insecure, and local-only logs are lost when devices are compromised."},
{"id":"n43","d":3,"q":"A logistics company is migrating its warehouse management system to IaaS. The application team wants to open SSH (TCP 22) from 0.0.0.0/0 to every virtual machine so that contractors can troubleshoot from wherever they are. The contractors' IP addresses change daily. What should the security architect recommend?","o":["Identity-aware bastion or session access, no open SSH","Move SSH to a non-standard high port and require 20-character passwords","Install fail2ban on every VM to block repeated failures","Allow SSH only from the contractors' current IP addresses, and have the operations team update the security group rules by hand each morning"],"a":[0],"e":"Identity-aware access (bastion or session manager) removes the internet-facing management surface and ties access to verified identity. Non-standard ports and fail2ban reduce noise but leave SSH exposed, and daily manual IP updates are error-prone and quickly go stale."},
{"id":"n44","d":3,"q":"An architect is assessing an organization's use of symmetric encryption. A legacy system encrypts each record with AES in ECB mode using a single key. Analysts noticed that identical records produce identical ciphertext. What is the BEST remediation?","o":["Re-encrypt every record using an authenticated mode such as AES-GCM, with a unique nonce generated for each record","Switch from AES-128 to AES-256 while keeping ECB mode, so that the key becomes much harder to brute-force","Compress each record before encrypting it in ECB mode","Keep ECB mode but rotate the key every month"],"a":[0],"e":"ECB encrypts identical blocks identically, leaking patterns no matter the key length. An authenticated mode like GCM, with unique nonces, hides patterns and detects tampering. Key size, compression, and rotation don't fix the mode's structural flaw."},
{"id":"n45","d":3,"q":"A team plans to use AES-GCM to encrypt messages between microservices. A developer suggests using a static nonce because 'the key is rotated every day anyway.' Why is this a serious problem?","o":["Nonce reuse under one GCM key exposes plaintext XORs and lets attackers forge authentication tags","GCM requires a static nonce, so the suggestion is correct","Nonce reuse only affects performance, because the cipher must recompute its key schedule for every message that is encrypted","Nonce reuse only matters for data at rest, not in transit"],"a":[0],"e":"GCM nonce reuse is catastrophic: it exposes keystream (XOR of plaintexts) and the GHASH authentication key, allowing forgeries. Daily rotation doesn't help, since thousands of messages a day share the key. Use random 96-bit nonces with volume limits or a counter."},
{"id":"n46","d":3,"q":"An enterprise uses a mixture of on-premises, multiple IaaS providers, and edge locations. The CISO wants consistent workload protection regardless of where the workload runs. Which approach BEST meets this requirement?","o":["A cross-environment CWPP","Each provider's native security tools, accepting that policies will differ","Repatriate all workloads to on-premises","Perimeter firewalls at each environment's edge, without host-level controls, since each provider already secures its own infrastructure"],"a":[0],"e":"CWPP tools are designed for hybrid and multicloud consistency. Native tools differ by provider, repatriating everything ignores business needs, and perimeter-only defense misses threats inside each environment."},
{"id":"n47","d":3,"q":"A hospital's network contains thousands of unmanaged medical and IoT devices, many running old operating systems that can't take agents. The security team doesn't know what most devices are. What should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Passive discovery, then segmentation","Install EDR agents on all medical devices before doing anything else","Run aggressive active vulnerability scans across the medical network so that every device is found as quickly as possible","Block any device not already in the CMDB until registered"],"a":[0],"e":"You can't protect what you don't know. Passive discovery is safe for fragile medical devices, unlike aggressive scanning, which can crash them. Agents often aren't supported, and blocking unknown devices could interrupt patient care."},
{"id":"n48","d":3,"q":"A company has users in its main office and wants to inspect encrypted web traffic for malware and data loss. Privacy counsel warns about decrypting employees' personal banking and health traffic. What is the BEST design?","o":["Selective TLS inspection with privacy bypasses","Decrypt and inspect all TLS traffic without exception, since the network and the devices belong to the company","Skip TLS inspection and rely only on DNS filtering","Decrypt everything but delete personal browsing logs nightly"],"a":[0],"e":"Selective decryption balances security visibility against privacy and legal risk, and transparency in policy is important. Decrypting everything raises legal and ethical problems, no inspection leaves blind spots, and deleting logs afterward doesn't undo the decryption."},
{"id":"n49","d":3,"q":"A company operates an internet-facing API with customer-specific rate limits. Attackers are using thousands of residential proxy IPs to scrape data at low per-IP rates. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Bot management using behavioral and device fingerprinting signals, combined with per-account and per-API-key rate limits","A static IP blocklist updated weekly from a commercial threat intelligence feed of known proxy and VPN exit nodes","Geo-blocking countries where the company doesn't operate","Raising per-IP limits so legitimate users are never blocked"],"a":[0],"e":"Distributed low-rate scraping evades IP-based controls. Behavioral bot detection and limits tied to identities (accounts, keys) are effective. Static blocklists and geo-blocking are easily bypassed through residential proxies."},
{"id":"n50","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing the protection of a cloud tenant's management plane. Which control MOST directly reduces the risk of a full environment takeover?","o":["Phishing-resistant MFA on top-level admin accounts, least privilege daily","Enable encryption at rest on every storage service in the tenant","Deploy a WAF in front of all public-facing applications","Turn on flow logging for every virtual network"],"a":[0],"e":"The management plane controls everything, so compromising a top-level admin means full takeover. Strong protection of those accounts and least-privilege daily roles reduce that risk most. Encryption, WAFs, and flow logs protect data and workloads, not the control plane."},
{"id":"n51","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing a file integrity monitoring solution for critical servers. Which design consideration is MOST important for the integrity of the monitoring itself?","o":["Off-host baselines and alerting","Run integrity checks every five minutes, whatever the performance impact on the production servers being monitored","Monitor every file, including temp and log files","Store baselines in a hidden directory on the same server"],"a":[0],"e":"If baselines live on the compromised host, the attacker can update them. Off-host storage and alerting preserve integrity. Monitoring everything creates noise, and overly frequent scans hurt performance without improving trust."},
{"id":"n52","d":3,"q":"A company wants to reduce the risk of attackers moving laterally from compromised workstations to servers. Which network design change has the GREATEST impact?","o":["Restrict workstation-to-workstation and admin protocol traffic","Add a second internet firewall in series at the edge","Enlarge workstation subnets to reduce routing rules","Deploy wireless intrusion prevention in the offices"],"a":[0],"e":"Most lateral movement uses SMB, RDP, WinRM, and similar protocols between workstations and to servers. Restricting those flows breaks common attack paths. Edge firewalls, subnet sizing, and WIPS don't affect internal lateral movement."},
{"id":"n53","d":3,"q":"An architect is evaluating hardware security for laptops that hold sensitive data. Which control MOST effectively protects data if a powered-off laptop is stolen?","o":["Full-disk encryption tied to the TPM with a pre-boot PIN","A BIOS password that must be entered before the operating system can begin to boot on the laptop","A strong Windows password with lockout after five attempts","Remote wipe through the MDM platform"],"a":[0],"e":"Full-disk encryption with TPM and PIN protects data at rest even if the drive is removed. BIOS and OS passwords can be bypassed by removing the disk, and remote wipe only works if the device comes online."},
{"id":"n54","d":3,"q":"A company stores encryption keys for its customer database in a cloud KMS. The architect wants to ensure that a compromised application server can't export the keys. Which design achieves this?","o":["Use the KMS encrypt and decrypt APIs so keys never leave the KMS, with IAM limiting which roles can call them","Download the key when the application starts and keep it in memory for performance","Store an encrypted copy of the key on disk, decrypted with a password held in a configuration file","Embed the key in the application source and obfuscate it at build time"],"a":[0],"e":"With non-exportable keys and API-based operations, a compromised server can at most call decrypt (which is logged and can be rate-limited); it can't steal the key. Keys in memory, on disk, or in code can all be extracted."},
{"id":"n55","d":3,"q":"A multinational company needs to provide secure internet access for 20,000 remote employees. The current design backhauls all traffic through headquarters, causing poor performance. Which design BEST improves performance while maintaining security?","o":["A cloud-delivered secure web gateway with points of presence near users, enforcing the same policies","Let remote users browse directly with no inspection","Double headquarters bandwidth and keep backhauling","Ship a firewall appliance to each employee's home and manage all 20,000 of them centrally from the headquarters security team"],"a":[0],"e":"Cloud SWGs inspect traffic close to the user with consistent policy, removing backhaul latency. No inspection loses protection, more bandwidth doesn't fix latency, and home appliances are impractical at scale."},
{"id":"n56","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing an architecture for secure software updates on 2 million consumer IoT devices. Which design is MOST important to prevent malicious firmware installation?","o":["Signed firmware verified by secure boot","Distribute firmware over HTTPS from a CDN, which guarantees integrity","Include a CRC32 checksum with each firmware file","Require users to press a button on the device to approve each update before it installs, so that no update can install without consent"],"a":[0],"e":"Code signing with protected keys plus device-side verification in a secure boot chain ensures only authentic firmware runs, and anti-rollback stops downgrades to vulnerable versions. HTTPS protects transport but not a compromised server, CRC isn't cryptographic, and user approval doesn't verify authenticity."},
{"id":"n57","d":3,"q":"An organization is designing its cloud landing zone. Which control MOST effectively prevents individual workload teams from disabling central security logging in their accounts?","o":["Organization-level preventive policies that deny stopping, deleting, or changing central audit trails","A written policy instructing workload teams never to disable logging in their own accounts","Monthly manual audits of each account's logging","Email alerts to security whenever logging is disabled"],"a":[0],"e":"Organization-level deny policies take effect even if an account admin has full rights, preventing the action. Policies, audits, and alerts are directive or detective; they react after logging is already off."},
{"id":"n58","d":3,"q":"A security architect needs to provide secure connectivity between an on-premises data center and a cloud VPC with high throughput and consistent latency. Data in transit must be encrypted. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Private interconnect plus MACsec or IPsec","An internet-based IPsec VPN alone, which provides consistent latency and high throughput","A private interconnect without encryption, since private circuits are inherently secure and isolated from other customers","Public internet with TLS on each application"],"a":[0],"e":"Private interconnects give consistent performance but aren't encrypted by default, so add MACsec or IPsec. Internet VPNs have variable latency, and 'private' doesn't mean encrypted."},
{"id":"n59","d":3,"q":"A company's architect is designing protection for a critical web application against the OWASP Top 10. The application can't be modified quickly. Which control provides the FASTEST risk reduction?","o":["Deploy a WAF with managed rules, tuned in detection mode before blocking","Rewrite the application in a memory-safe language before the next scheduled release","Schedule a yearly penetration test","Train developers in secure coding and wait for future releases to fix the vulnerable code paths"],"a":[0],"e":"A WAF can be deployed quickly as a compensating control while the code is fixed. Rewrites take time, annual testing finds but doesn't fix, and training helps future code but not the current exposure."},
{"id":"n60","d":3,"q":"A security architect is reviewing the encryption design for a messaging app. The design uses RSA key transport: the client encrypts a session key with the server's long-term RSA public key. What is the MOST significant weakness of this design?","o":["It lacks forward secrecy","RSA can't be used for key transport at all","RSA key transport is slower than AES for bulk data","It requires certificates on every client device"],"a":[0],"e":"Static RSA key transport means one private key protects every past session. Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman (ECDHE) provides forward secrecy, and TLS 1.3 removed static RSA for this reason."},
{"id":"n61","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing a secure architecture for a SCADA system that uses Modbus, a protocol with no authentication. Replacing the protocol is not feasible. Which compensating control is MOST effective?","o":["Zones and conduits with protocol-aware filtering and OT monitoring","Encrypt all Modbus traffic with TLS on the PLCs","Move SCADA onto the corporate network to use its controls","Change Modbus to a non-standard port number"],"a":[0],"e":"Legacy OT protocols lack security, so the architecture must isolate them and enforce allowed operations with protocol-aware firewalls, plus monitoring. Many PLCs can't do TLS, the corporate network increases exposure, and port changes are obscurity."},
{"id":"n62","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing an email security architecture. The organization has seen a surge in business email compromise (BEC) using look-alike domains. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Look-alike detection plus out-of-band payment checks","DMARC at p=reject on the organization's own domain","S/MIME encryption of all outbound email","Blocking all attachments from external senders, since business email compromise messages almost always carry malicious files"],"a":[0],"e":"DMARC protects your exact domain but not look-alikes. BEC is fundamentally a process attack, so look-alike detection, external tagging, and out-of-band verification of payment changes are the strongest controls. Encryption and attachment blocking don't address BEC, which is usually text only."},
{"id":"n63","d":3,"q":"An organization is moving from a perimeter-based model to zero trust. Which component is responsible for continuously evaluating trust and making access decisions based on policy, according to NIST SP 800-207?","o":["The policy engine, which together with the policy administrator forms the policy decision point in SP 800-207","The policy enforcement point","The data access policy repository","The continuous diagnostics and mitigation system, which reports the security state of each enterprise asset"],"a":[0],"e":"In SP 800-207, the policy engine makes the decision and the policy administrator executes it by configuring the PEP. Together they form the PDP. The PEP enforces, and CDM and data policies are inputs to the engine."},
{"id":"n64","d":3,"q":"A company's data center uses a raised floor with under-floor cabling and a gas-based fire suppression system. Which consideration is MOST important for personnel safety?","o":["Pre-discharge alarms, abort switches, evacuation procedures, and a suppression agent that is safe for occupied spaces","Replace the gas system with water sprinklers, since water is always safer for both people and electronics","Disable suppression during business hours","Install suppression only in the network closet"],"a":[0],"e":"Life safety always comes first. Gas systems can create hazardous atmospheres, so alarms, abort switches, and evacuation procedures are critical. Clean agents such as FM-200 or Novec 1230 are safer for occupied spaces than CO2."},
{"id":"n65","d":3,"q":"An insurance company runs a hybrid environment. Its security team has discovered that several developers created cloud access keys with full administrator rights two years ago and embedded them in scripts on their laptops. None of the keys have been rotated. What is the BEST long-term architectural fix?","o":["Federated short-lived credentials, blocking long-lived admin keys","Rotate the existing keys every 90 days and email new keys to each developer","Scan developer laptops weekly and delete any keys found","Store the existing keys in an encrypted spreadsheet on a shared drive"],"a":[0],"e":"The root problem is long-lived, over-privileged static credentials. Federation with short-lived tokens removes that class of risk, and preventive policy stops recurrence. Rotation and scanning reduce exposure but keep the pattern, and an encrypted spreadsheet just moves the keys around."},
{"id":"n66","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing a network for a new office. The design team suggests running VoIP, guest Wi-Fi, building management systems, and corporate devices on one flat network to reduce cost. What is the PRIMARY risk of this design?","o":["Compromise of any low-trust device gives direct access to corporate systems","VoIP call quality will degrade because broadcast traffic from guest devices and building controllers competes with voice traffic","The switches will run out of MAC address table space as guest and IoT devices join","Guest users will consume most of the available internet bandwidth"],"a":[0],"e":"Mixing trust levels on a flat network allows lateral movement from the weakest device. Segmentation by trust level (corporate, guest, VoIP, building systems) contains compromise. Performance concerns are real but secondary to security."},
{"id":"n67","d":3,"q":"An organization is evaluating hardware security modules for its payment processing. Which property of an HSM is MOST important for protecting PIN encryption keys?","o":["Tamper-responsive hardware that zeroizes keys, validated to PCI PTS HSM or FIPS 140-3 Level 3","High throughput for bulk encryption of large files, so that batch payment files can be processed quickly at the end of the day","Support for the widest possible range of algorithms","Lower cost than software-based key management"],"a":[0],"e":"HSMs for payment keys must resist and respond to physical tampering, and PCI requires validated devices. Throughput, algorithm breadth, and cost matter but don't define the protection the keys need."},
{"id":"n68","d":3,"q":"A security architect discovers that many internal TLS certificates expired unexpectedly last quarter, causing outages. Certificates are tracked in a spreadsheet. Which architecture change BEST addresses the root cause?","o":["Automated certificate discovery, inventory, and renewal (for example, ACME), with expiry monitoring","Issue internal certificates with ten-year validity so they almost never expire","Assign one engineer to check the spreadsheet every week","Replace the internal CA with self-signed certificates, which teams can create themselves without waiting for the PKI team to respond"],"a":[0],"e":"Manual tracking doesn't scale. Automated discovery and renewal fixes the root cause. Long validity increases compromise risk and is rejected by many clients, and self-signed certificates undermine trust."},
{"id":"n69","d":3,"q":"A healthcare provider plans to host a patient portal in a public cloud. A business associate agreement (BAA) is in place with the provider. Which statement about responsibilities is MOST accurate?","o":["The hospital still owns its configuration and PHI controls","The BAA transfers all of the healthcare provider's HIPAA responsibilities to the cloud provider for anything hosted on its platform","The provider will configure the portal's encryption and access control","With a BAA in place, no HIPAA risk analysis is required"],"a":[0],"e":"A BAA is necessary for HIPAA but doesn't shift customer responsibilities. Under shared responsibility, the customer secures its configuration, identities, and data, and a HIPAA risk analysis is still required."},
{"id":"n70","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing a security architecture for a global e-commerce site that must remain available during regional outages. Which design BEST supports both availability and security?","o":["Active-active regions with security deployed as code","A single region using larger instances to handle peak load","Active-passive, with relaxed controls in the passive region to save cost","Multiple regions, each built and configured manually by a different operations team according to its own local runbooks"],"a":[0],"e":"Active-active across regions removes single points of failure, and IaC ensures identical security everywhere. Weaker controls in the standby region become the attacker's target, and manual configuration causes drift."},
{"id":"n71","d":3,"q":"An organization uses virtual desktop infrastructure (VDI) for contractors. Which security benefit is MOST significant?","o":["Data stays in the data center, and copy, paste, and file transfer can be restricted by policy","Contractors no longer need to authenticate, because the virtual desktop session itself proves that they are authorized users","Contractor endpoints become immune to malware","VDI encrypts the contractor's personal files"],"a":[0],"e":"VDI centralizes data and lets policies restrict data movement. Authentication is still needed, contractor endpoints can still be compromised (keyloggers, screen capture), and VDI doesn't protect personal files."},
{"id":"n72","d":3,"q":"A company's security architect is evaluating network detection and response (NDR). In an environment where most traffic is encrypted with TLS 1.3, which capability remains MOST useful without decryption?","o":["Metadata and behavior analysis: flows, JA4 fingerprints, beaconing, DNS, and connection graphs","Signature matching against HTTP request and response payloads","Extracting attachments from email traffic in transit","Inspecting the full server certificate chain in each TLS 1.3 handshake to identify connections to malicious infrastructure"],"a":[0],"e":"Without decryption, NDR relies on metadata and behavior: who talks to whom, how often, fingerprints, and DNS. Payload signatures need plaintext, and TLS 1.3 encrypts most of the handshake, including server certificates."},
{"id":"n73","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing database security for a system used by a call center. Agents should see only the last four digits of customer identification numbers, while fraud investigators see full numbers. Which control BEST implements this?","o":["Role-based dynamic data masking","Transparent data encryption of the whole database, with separate keys for the call center and the fraud team","Separate copies of the database for each user group","Column encryption with one key shared by all users"],"a":[0],"e":"Dynamic masking shows different views by role without duplicating data. TDE protects only storage media, separate copies cause sync and sprawl problems, and a shared encryption key doesn't differentiate access."},
{"id":"n74","d":3,"q":"A security architect is planning for crypto-agility across the enterprise. Which practice MOST directly enables the organization to replace algorithms quickly when weaknesses are found?","o":["A crypto inventory plus centrally managed, configurable crypto services","Hard-code the strongest available algorithms in each application","Use a different algorithm in every application for diversity","Rely on vendors to tell the organization when an algorithm becomes weak"],"a":[0],"e":"Crypto-agility needs knowing where cryptography is used (inventory) and being able to change it without rewriting applications (abstraction). Hard-coding, diversity for its own sake, and waiting for vendors all slow migration."},
{"id":"n75","d":3,"q":"A company is deploying a new Wi-Fi network for its corporate offices. Which authentication method provides the STRONGEST protection against credential theft through rogue access points?","o":["WPA3-Enterprise using EAP-TLS with device certificates and strict server certificate validation","WPA2-Personal with a long passphrase that is changed every quarter and shared only with employees","WPA2-Enterprise with PEAP-MSCHAPv2 and no server certificate validation","An open network with a captive portal that checks Active Directory credentials"],"a":[0],"e":"EAP-TLS uses certificates on both sides, so there's no password to phish, and validating the server certificate prevents rogue AP attacks. PEAP-MSCHAPv2 without validation lets rogue APs capture crackable hashes, and PSKs are shared secrets."},
{"id":"n76","d":3,"q":"An organization wants to secure DNS queries from its managed endpoints and block access to malicious domains, even when users are off the corporate network. Which design is MOST effective?","o":["Protective DNS enforced on the endpoint wherever it is","An internal DNS blocklist, relying on users to connect to the VPN whenever they are working outside the office","Blocking DNS over HTTPS at the corporate firewall","Public DNS resolvers without filtering"],"a":[0],"e":"Endpoint-enforced protective DNS works on any network. Internal-only filtering fails when users are off VPN, blocking DoH at the firewall doesn't help off-network, and unfiltered public resolvers give no protection."},
{"id":"n77","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing a secure configuration baseline for 5,000 Linux servers. Which approach provides the MOST consistent and auditable results?","o":["Baseline as code with continuous drift detection","A Word document describing the baseline, applied manually by administrators","Apply the baseline once at build time and rely on change management","Let each administrator choose the settings appropriate to their servers"],"a":[0],"e":"Baselines as code with continuous drift detection give consistency and audit evidence. Manual application and build-time-only approaches drift over time, and per-admin choice gives no baseline at all."},
{"id":"n78","d":3,"q":"A university wants to give researchers high-speed data transfer for large datasets while protecting the campus network. Traditional firewalls create a performance bottleneck. Which architecture is MOST appropriate?","o":["A Science DMZ with dedicated transfer nodes, router ACLs, and monitoring","Remove the campus firewall entirely so that research transfers are no longer slowed by stateful inspection","Upgrade the campus firewall to the largest model available","Have researchers ship hard drives instead"],"a":[0],"e":"The Science DMZ model uses dedicated, hardened transfer nodes with ACLs and monitoring rather than stateful inspection, giving high throughput while isolating the campus network. Removing the firewall exposes everything, and big firewalls may still bottleneck."},
{"id":"n79","d":3,"q":"A company wants to reduce the risk of attackers using compromised credentials to access its SaaS applications from unknown devices. Which architecture control is MOST effective?","o":["Conditional access requiring managed, compliant devices plus MFA","A longer minimum password length for all SaaS accounts, combined with screening against known breached passwords","IP allow-listing to the corporate office network","Monthly phishing awareness training"],"a":[0],"e":"Conditional access with device compliance means stolen credentials alone don't work from an attacker's device. Longer passwords don't stop phishing, IP allow-lists break remote work, and training reduces but doesn't eliminate credential theft."},
{"id":"n80","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing a secure storage design for audit logs that must be retained for seven years and must be provably unaltered. Which design is BEST?","o":["WORM storage in compliance mode with hash chaining or signed digests","A standard file server with daily backups kept for seven years in an offsite vault","AES-256 encryption on a shared drive","Compressed logs on administrators' workstations"],"a":[0],"e":"WORM storage in compliance mode prevents modification or deletion for the retention period, even by admins, and hash chains or signed digests show integrity. Backups and encryption don't prove logs weren't altered."},
{"id":"n81","d":3,"q":"A company uses a cloud provider's serverless functions for payment processing. Which security responsibility does the customer retain?","o":["Code, dependencies, roles, secrets, and input validation","Patching the operating system and language runtime that the functions run on","Physical security of the provider's data centers","Managing the hypervisor or micro-VM layer that isolates one customer's function instances from another's"],"a":[0],"e":"In serverless the provider manages the OS, runtime, and infrastructure. The customer still owns code, dependencies, permissions, secrets, and data handling, where most serverless incidents happen."},
{"id":"n82","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing remote access for third-party vendors who support industrial control systems. Which design MOST effectively limits risk?","o":["Brokered, approved, recorded, time-limited vendor sessions","A permanent site-to-site VPN from each vendor's network directly into the control network","A shared vendor account on an always-on jump server","Let vendors install their own remote tools"],"a":[0],"e":"Vendor access is a top OT attack vector. Brokered, approved, time-limited, recorded sessions minimize exposure and give accountability. Persistent VPNs, shared accounts, and vendor-installed tools create uncontrolled entry points."},
{"id":"n83","d":3,"q":"An organization is designing physical access control for a new headquarters. Which design BEST prevents tailgating into secure areas?","o":["Mantraps or security turnstiles with anti-passback","Badge readers on every door, with a written policy prohibiting employees from holding doors open for others","CCTV at entrances reviewed after incidents","A guard at the main lobby entrance only"],"a":[0],"e":"Mantraps and turnstiles physically enforce one person per credential, and anti-passback stops badge sharing. Policies and CCTV are directive or detective, and a lobby guard doesn't cover internal secure areas."},
{"id":"n84","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing protection for an organization's container images. Which control MOST directly prevents untrusted images from being deployed?","o":["Sign images in the pipeline and verify signatures at admission","Scan images for vulnerabilities after deployment and alert the owning team about any critical findings","Use the 'latest' tag so images are always current","Allow pulls from any public registry"],"a":[0],"e":"Signing plus admission-time verification ensures only trusted, unmodified images run. Post-deployment scanning is detective, 'latest' tags are mutable and unpredictable, and public registries carry supply chain risk."},
{"id":"n85","d":3,"q":"An architect is assessing a design where all corporate laptops connect through an always-on VPN. Users complain that collaboration tools perform poorly. The network team proposes split tunneling for those tools. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Targeted split tunneling for trusted SaaS only","Full split tunneling for all traffic to maximize performance","Deny split tunneling and ask users to accept the slowdown","Disconnect the VPN during meetings"],"a":[0],"e":"Targeted split tunneling for trusted, high-bandwidth SaaS improves performance while endpoint controls and cloud filtering keep protection. Full split tunneling loses visibility, and ignoring the problem leads users to find workarounds."},
{"id":"n86","d":3,"q":"A media company streams live events to millions of viewers. During a major event, attackers launched an application-layer DDoS consisting of slow, legitimate-looking HTTPS requests to the login page, exhausting backend database connections. Network-layer DDoS protection did not trigger. Which control would have been MOST effective?","o":["Layer 7 protection at the CDN or WAF, with per-endpoint rate limits and challenges","A larger network-layer scrubbing contract with the upstream provider","More database connections and read replicas","Geo-blocking all countries outside the main market"],"a":[0],"e":"Application-layer attacks look like legitimate traffic and need L7 controls: per-endpoint rate limits, bot challenges, and protecting expensive backends. Network scrubbing doesn't see them, bigger connection pools just delay exhaustion, and geo-blocking is easily bypassed."},
{"id":"n87","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing encryption for a backup system that sends data to a third-party cloud storage provider. The organization's policy states that the provider must never be able to read backup data. Which design meets this?","o":["Client-side encryption with keys held in the organization's own KMS or HSM","Server-side encryption at the storage provider, using keys that the provider generates, stores, and rotates on the customer's behalf","TLS encryption during upload","A provider region in the organization's own country"],"a":[0],"e":"Only client-side encryption with organization-held keys keeps the provider from seeing plaintext. Server-side encryption with provider keys and TLS in transit both leave plaintext accessible to the provider, and region choice doesn't affect access."},
{"id":"n88","d":3,"q":"An organization runs a Kubernetes cluster shared by several product teams. A team's compromised pod was able to reach the database pods of another team. Which control MOST directly prevents this?","o":["Default-deny network policies between namespaces","Separate node pools per team, so that each team's pods are scheduled only on that team's own set of worker nodes","RBAC on the Kubernetes API by namespace","Image vulnerability scanning before deployment"],"a":[0],"e":"By default, Kubernetes allows all pod-to-pod traffic. Network policies with default deny enforce isolation at the network layer. API RBAC controls management actions, not pod traffic, and separate node pools don't block traffic either. Image scanning reduces compromise likelihood but doesn't contain it."},
{"id":"n89","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing secrets management for a cloud-native application. Developers currently store database passwords in environment variables defined in source-controlled deployment files. What is the BEST improvement?","o":["A secrets manager issuing short-lived credentials at runtime via workload identity","Base64-encode the passwords in the deployment files so they aren't stored in readable form","Move the passwords to a separate private repository","Encrypt them with a key stored in the same repository"],"a":[0],"e":"A secrets manager with workload identity and dynamic credentials removes static secrets from code and limits their lifetime. Base64 is encoding, not encryption, a private repo still exposes secrets to everyone with access, and storing the key next to the ciphertext defeats encryption."},
{"id":"n90","d":3,"q":"A company uses an SD-WAN solution. The security architect learns that the SD-WAN orchestrator is accessible from the internet with single-factor authentication. What is the MOST significant risk?","o":["An attacker could push malicious configuration to every branch device","Branch internet performance could degrade during peak hours because the orchestrator would be overloaded by login attempts","The orchestrator's license could be exceeded","Branch users could bypass web filtering"],"a":[0],"e":"Centralized orchestration means a compromised controller affects every site. It should be restricted to management networks or protected with strong MFA, and monitored closely."},
{"id":"n91","d":3,"q":"An organization wants to protect sensitive documents stored in a SaaS collaboration platform from being shared externally by mistake. Which approach is MOST effective?","o":["Sensitivity labels enforced by platform policy and API-based CASB controls on external sharing","A policy instructing employees not to share sensitive documents with anyone outside the company","Network DLP at the corporate firewall","Encrypting employee laptops"],"a":[0],"e":"Sharing happens inside the SaaS platform, so controls must work there: labels plus platform and CASB policies. Network DLP doesn't see SaaS-internal sharing, and laptop encryption doesn't affect cloud sharing."},
{"id":"n92","d":3,"q":"A security architect is evaluating a proposal to use IPv6 throughout a new data center. Which security consideration is MOST important?","o":["IPv6 policy parity plus IPv6-specific defenses","IPv6 has mandatory built-in encryption, so additional security controls aren't needed for internal traffic","IPv6 address space is too large to scan, so no firewall is needed","Disable IPv6, because it can't be secured"],"a":[0],"e":"Many organizations forget to secure IPv6, creating a parallel unprotected path. Policy parity and IPv6-specific defenses such as RA Guard and DHCPv6 Guard are critical. IPsec support was once mandatory to implement but is not mandatory to use, and large address spaces don't replace firewalls."},
{"id":"n93","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing a backup strategy for a critical application. Which strategy MOST closely follows the 3-2-1-1-0 rule for ransomware resilience?","o":["Three copies, two media, one offsite, one immutable or air-gapped, zero restore errors","One copy on a local disk array with daily snapshots retained for 30 days and replicated to a second array in the same room","Two copies on the same SAN with RAID 10","Three copies in the same cloud region and account"],"a":[0],"e":"3-2-1-1-0: three copies, two media types, one offsite, one immutable or offline, zero errors after verification. The other options share failure domains or lack immutability and testing."},
{"id":"n94","d":3,"q":"An organization is concerned about attackers exfiltrating data through DNS tunneling. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Centralize DNS and analyze queries for anomalies","Block all DNS traffic at the perimeter firewall","Sign the organization's own zones with DNSSEC","Increase the TTL on internal DNS records"],"a":[0],"e":"Centralizing DNS and analyzing queries detects tunneling. Blocking all DNS breaks the network, DNSSEC protects authenticity (not exfiltration), and TTL is irrelevant."},
{"id":"n95","d":3,"q":"A company wants to deploy a honeynet to detect attackers who have penetrated its network. Which design consideration is MOST important?","o":["Isolate it so it can't be used to attack production, while monitoring all activity","Connect it directly to production databases so that it looks realistic to an attacker exploring the network","Place it on the internet without monitoring","Seed it with real employee credentials"],"a":[0],"e":"A honeynet must be contained so attackers can't pivot from it, and monitored, since that's its purpose. Real data or credentials in it create real risk."},
{"id":"n96","d":3,"q":"An organization has adopted IaC for all cloud deployments. Which control MOST effectively prevents insecure configurations from reaching production?","o":["Policy-as-code checks on IaC in the pipeline that block violating deployments","A manual review of the cloud console after each deployment, performed by the cloud platform team within one business day","A quarterly cloud penetration test","Developer attestations that configurations are secure"],"a":[0],"e":"Scanning IaC before deployment catches misconfigurations early and consistently. Manual review and pen tests are after the fact, and signed statements don't enforce anything."},
{"id":"n97","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing secure connectivity for a partner who needs to access one internal API. The partner's network security is unknown. Which design is BEST?","o":["Expose only the API through a gateway with strong auth","A site-to-site VPN giving the partner access to the internal network","Expose the API to the internet without authentication on a non-standard port","Share a VPN account with the partner"],"a":[0],"e":"Exposing only the specific API through a gateway with strong authentication and scoped permissions limits the partner's access to what's needed. A VPN grants network access, and the other options lack authentication or accountability."},
{"id":"n98","d":3,"q":"An architect must decide where to terminate TLS for a web application hosted behind a load balancer in the cloud. The application handles highly sensitive financial data, and compliance requires encryption in transit everywhere. What is the BEST design?","o":["Terminate at the load balancer, then re-encrypt to the backends","Terminate TLS at the load balancer and send plaintext to the backend instances, since traffic inside the VPC is private","Use TLS only on the login page","Disable TLS and encrypt selected fields in the app"],"a":[0],"e":"Re-encryption keeps traffic encrypted inside the VPC, meeting 'everywhere' requirements while allowing load balancer features. Private networks still carry insider and lateral risks."},
{"id":"n99","d":3,"q":"A company's server room uses a single air conditioning unit and a single UPS. The BIA rates the room's systems as critical. Which design change BEST addresses environmental risk?","o":["N+1 cooling and power (UPS and generator), with environmental monitoring and alerting","A second server room on a different floor, with the same single air conditioning unit and UPS design as the first","More fire extinguishers in the server room","Moving the servers to employees' desks"],"a":[0],"e":"Critical systems need redundant power and cooling plus monitoring. Fire extinguishers address fire, not power or cooling. Another unprotected room has the same weaknesses."},
{"id":"n100","d":3,"q":"A security architect is selecting a digital signature algorithm for long-term document signing (documents must be verifiable for 20 years). Which consideration is MOST important?","o":["Long-term validation and post-quantum readiness","MD5 with RSA, because it is widely supported by document readers","The shortest possible keys to keep signatures small","Relying on the signer's certificate remaining valid for the full 20 years, with revocation checked at verification time"],"a":[0],"e":"Long-term signatures need trusted timestamps and archival formats (such as PAdES-LTA) that can be refreshed as algorithms weaken, plus quantum readiness. MD5 is broken, short keys are weak, and certificates rarely last 20 years."},
{"id":"n101","d":3,"q":"An organization uses jump servers for administrators. An attacker compromised one jump server and harvested credentials of every administrator who logged in. Which redesign BEST prevents this?","o":["PAM with credential injection and JIT privilege","Add antivirus to the jump server","Require more complex jump server passwords","Add a second jump server for redundancy"],"a":[0],"e":"Credential injection keeps target passwords off the jump host and out of administrators' hands, so harvesting fails. JIT limits standing privilege. Antivirus and password complexity don't stop credential theft from a compromised host."},
{"id":"n102","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing a secure architecture for an organization's internal wiki that contains low-sensitivity information. The team proposes the same controls as for the payment system. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Controls proportionate to the data's classification, using the low-sensitivity baseline","Payment-grade controls for everything, so that every system in the organization is protected consistently and audits are simpler","No controls, since the data is low sensitivity","Whatever controls the team prefers"],"a":[0],"e":"Controls should match risk. Over-protecting low-value assets wastes resources, and under-protecting creates exposure. Classification-driven baselines provide consistency without overspending."},
{"id":"n103","d":3,"q":"A company wants to use the public cloud for burst computing but must ensure that sensitive data processed there is not recoverable after instances are terminated. Which control BEST addresses data remanence?","o":["Crypto-erase via customer-managed keys","Ask the provider to physically shred the disks after each use and provide a certificate of destruction for every instance","Overwrite volumes with zeros seven times before termination","Avoid disks and process everything in memory"],"a":[0],"e":"In multi-tenant cloud you can't control physical media. Crypto-erase (destroying keys) makes data unrecoverable and is recognized in NIST SP 800-88. Providers won't shred disks per customer, multi-pass overwrite isn't reliable on virtualized or SSD storage, and memory-only isn't practical for most workloads."},
{"id":"n104","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing the architecture for a mobile banking app. Which control MOST effectively protects against man-in-the-middle attacks using rogue certificates installed on a user's device?","o":["Certificate or public key pinning, with a backup pin","Standard HTTPS with any certificate that chains to a CA trusted by the device's operating system certificate store","Obfuscation of the app's code","A strong login password requirement"],"a":[0],"e":"Pinning makes the app accept only expected keys, defeating rogue or compromised CAs and interception proxies. Plain HTTPS trusts any CA in the device store, which an attacker or malware may have modified. Obfuscation and passwords don't stop MITM."},
{"id":"n105","d":3,"q":"A company is designing network access for guests in its offices. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["An isolated guest SSID with internet-only access, client isolation, and bandwidth limits","Guest access on the corporate network with a separate guest password that changes weekly and is printed at reception","Guest access on the corporate network with MAC filtering","No guest network; guests use employees' laptops"],"a":[0],"e":"Guests should be fully isolated from corporate resources, with client isolation to prevent guest-to-guest attacks. Corporate network access, even with a password or MAC filter, exposes internal systems, and sharing employee laptops is worse."}
);
